1. Context assembly. The single biggest lever, and it's not model work. The reply can only be as good as the facts you hand it. This is why Thread.context is load-bearing on both sides — for the evaluator it's the closed world that makes faithfulness objective, and for the generator it's the retrieval target. Bad reply usually means bad context, not a bad model.
2. Prompt structure. Role, tone constraints, an explicit "assert nothing outside the provided facts; if a fact is missing, say you'll follow up." That last clause alone kills a large share of faithfulness failures — you give the model a legal escape hatch instead of forcing it to guess.
3. Best-of-N with the evaluator as the filter. Generate k=4 replies at temperature, run gates on each, discard anything that fails, rank survivors by the scored tier, surface the top one. This is the piece your reward-model outline was reaching for — same architecture, evaluator in the reward model's slot. No training, works on day one.
4. Self-revision loop. Feed a failed reply back with its gate failure attached: "you asserted a 14-day refund window; that fact is not in the thread. Rewrite." One retry catches most of it. Two has diminishing returns.
5. Learned reward model / fine-tuning. Only after you have thousands of logged accept/reject events from real usage. Not reachable here, and not reachable before the product ships.

Note what 3 and 4 mean: the evaluator is the quality system. It's not a separate grading exercise bolted on afterward — it's the filter and the critic. That's the actual argument for building it first, and it's a stronger framing than "evaluator is the deliverable." A generator without a trustworthy evaluator has no way to tell its good outputs from its bad ones, and no way to improve except vibes.


